Title: "Set Constructions"
Id: "b49297d0-a6d3-4843-85a0-2241c30b5a05"


Text: "Equipped with the core axioms of Zermelo--Fraenkel theory, we can
       construct the canonical objects of elementary mathematics. Specializing
       the Axiom of Pairing to identical arguments produces a set containing
       exactly one member, establishing the vital ontological distinction
       between an object $X$ and the collection $\{X\}$ containing it:"
Id: "032576ab-7fc3-4075-b24a-cd06583d18cd"


[\singleton.set:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
using: U is \set
when: X "in" U
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff: Z = X
Documented:
. called: "The singleton set of $X?$"
. written: "\left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The atomic unit $\{X\}$ containing precisely $X$ as its sole
                member. Distinguishes elementhood from containment
                ($X \in \{X\}$ versus $X \subseteq \{X\}$) and serves as the
                terminal object in the category of pointed sets."
Id: "08f35257-d0a7-42af-9866-7d138c56fc4c"


Text: "Applying pairing to distinct objects yields an unordered pair whose
       identity, by Extensionality, is completely symmetric and independent of
       listing order:"
Id: "c128a6a8-b892-4177-8622-29ba3cfa473a"


[\unordered.pair:of{x, y}]
Defines: Z is \set
using: U is \set
when: x, y "in" U
expresses:
. let: z is \set
  then:
  . have: z "in"? Z
    iff: (.z = x.) \.or./ (.z = y.)
Documented:
. called: "unordered pair of $x?$ and $y?$"
. written: "\left \{ x?, y? \right \}"
. description: "The symmetrical binary ensemble $\{x, y\} = \{y, x\}$.
                Extensionality renders element sequencing completely
                transparent, serving as the raw material for Kuratowski ordered
                pairs."
Id: "17401045-fc72-4bd4-af7d-ab578690f81c"


Text: "Adjoining a set to its own elements produces its successor. In 1923, John
       von Neumann realized that this inductive operation provides the exact
       mechanism for realizing the natural numbers as finite ordinals, where
       each number is the set of all predecessors:"
Id: "f49b05f5-b826-45d0-b327-b248946e28fc"


[\set.successor:of{X}]
Defines: Y is \set
when: X is \set
expresses:
. let: Z is \set
  then:
  . have: Z "in"? Y
    iff:
    . (.Z "in"? X.) \.or./ (.Z = X.)
Documented:
. called: "The set successor of $X?$"
. written: "X? \cup \left \{ X? \right \}"
. description: "The von Neumann inductive successor $S(X) = X \cup \{X\}$.
                Generates the finite von Neumann ordinals $\omega$, where each
                integer is the well-ordered set of its predecessors."
Id: "002b9914-ad06-4f5a-a3fb-803bd86e6c4b"


SectionTitle: "Set-Builder Notation"
Id: "55851b31-9fa9-4b8e-b61b-315b7559c66d"


Text: "In mathematical practice, sets are rarely defined by explicit
       enumeration. Instead, the Axiom Schema of Separation allows us to carve
       out subcollections using set properties: formulas that assign a definite
       truth value to any candidate element:"
Id: "b218d7dd-c01a-476d-9975-b05de57b8b8f"


[\set.property(X_)]
Declares: phi(X_) is (_ is \set) -> (_ is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "set property"
. description: "A first-order definable property $\varphi(X)$ in the language of
                set theory. Used by the Axiom Schema of Separation (Zermelo) to
                isolate bounded subcollections $\{x \in A : \varphi(x)\}$,
                avoiding unrestricted comprehension paradoxes."
Id: "cc680a81-3338-4848-8b9d-fcf368dd759e"


Text: "When an extensional list is desired, a finite collection can be defined
       directly by listing its coordinates:"
Id: "2d5cffd6-d094-4eac-99b1-e2c05b5557b9"


[\set:of{x[i_ := 1...n]}]
Defines: X is \set
when: x[1...n] is \\anything
expresses:
. let: y is \\anything
  then:
  . have: y "in"? X
    iff:
    . anyOf:
      . x[1...n] = y
Documented:
. called: "set of $\left \{ x? \right \}$"
. written: "\left \{ x? \right \}"
. description: "Finite extensional listing $\{x_1, \dots, x_n\}$ defined by the
                disjunction of coordinate identities. Forms the standard roster
                notation for finite sets."
Id: "61c901bc-5f36-479b-a32c-c37b7f98a9fb"


SectionTitle: "Ordered Pairs and Products"
Id: "b61f3f41-454a-4ea1-a69c-35fe7fb2ceb3"


Text: "While sets are inherently symmetric and unordered, mathematics
       fundamentally requires directional structures where the first component
       is distinguished from the second. In 1921, Kazimierz Kuratowski showed
       that ordered pairs can be encoded within pure set theory by nesting
       singletons and pairs:"
Id: "da7d91b3-693b-4ca2-ac2a-e94555b18c5f"


[\pair:of{x, y}]
Defines: p := \set:of{\set:of{x}, \set:of{x, y}} is \set
when: x, y is \\anything
Documented:
. called: "pair of $x?$ and $y?$"
. written: "\langle x?, y? \rangle"
. description: "Kuratowski's set-theoretic encoding
                $(x, y) = \{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\}$. Transforms asymmetric positional
                ordering into symmetric set containment, enabling the formal
                foundation of Cartesian products, relations, and functions."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{35}
Id: "8fd9d494-2882-4639-bb50-44a56626d1c4"


Text: "With ordered pairs available, we can gather all pairs formed by choosing
       a first coordinate from $A$ and a second coordinate from $B$. Named for
       René Descartes, the Cartesian product provides the foundational domain
       for relations, multivariable calculus, and operations:"
Id: "331daec2-edc7-4316-8fb6-0c801c3692ed"


[A \.set.cross./ B]
Defines: X := \set@{(a_, b_) : a_ "in" A; b_ "in" B}
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "Cartesian product of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \times B?"
. description: "The categorical product $A \times B$ in Set, collecting all
                ordered pairs $(a, b)$ for $a \in A$ and $b \in B$. Acts as the
                universal domain for binary operations, relations, and
                multivariable maps."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{35}
Id: "3bdd9cfb-f836-4d44-880a-873bb772cdef"


Text: "The legitimacy of Kuratowski's encoding rests entirely on whether it
       satisfies the characteristic property of ordered pairs: two pairs are
       identical if and only if their respective coordinates coincide:"
Id: "7ff735fd-e6ea-4af7-b4f6-df89e3068b6d"


Theorem:
given: x, y, u, v is \set
then: \pair:of{x, y} = \pair:of{u, v}
iff:
. x = u
. y = v
Documented:
. called: "coordinate equality for ordered pairs"
. description: "The fundamental theorem of ordered pairs:
                $(x, y) = (u, v) \iff (x = u \land y = v)$. Guarantees that
                Kuratowski's encoding faithfully captures positional asymmetry
                without information loss."
Proof: """If $x = u$ and $y = v$, then
          $\{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\} = \{\{u\}, \{u, v\}\}$ trivially. Conversely,
          suppose $\{\{x\}, \{x, y\}\} = \{\{u\}, \{u, v\}\}$. The intersection
          of all members of the left set is $\{x\}$, and of the right set is
          $\{u\}$, which forces $\{x\} = \{u\}$ and hence $x = u$. Now consider
          two cases:
          1. If $x = y$, then the left set is $\{\{x\}, \{x\}\} = \{\{x\}\}$,
             meaning the right set must also be a singleton. Thus
             $\{u\} = \{u, v\}$, which forces $u = v$, yielding $x = y = u = v$.
          2. If $x \neq y$, then the left set contains a unique two-element
             member $\{x, y\}$, which must equal the unique two-element member
             $\{u, v\}$ on the right. Since $x = u$ and $x \neq y$, it follows
             that $y = v$."""
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{35}
Id: "31691bb2-e8e1-4218-be4f-759add2b7ab7"


Text: "Because $(A \times B) \times C$ consists of pairs of the form
       $((a, b), c)$ whereas $A \times (B \times C)$ consists of $(a, (b, c))$,
       the Cartesian product is not strictly associative as a set-theoretic
       operation. However, the two products are naturally and canonically
       isomorphic via coordinate regrouping:"
Id: "cd332b77-e7cc-4e59-92e6-40decc503be9"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
then:
. exists: phi(x__) is \function:on{(.(.A \.set.cross./ B.) \.set.cross./ C.)}:to{(.A \.set.cross./ (.B \.set.cross./ C.).)}
  suchThat:
  . phi is? \(bijective)::function:?on{(.(.A \.set.cross./ B.) \.set.cross./ C.)}:?to{(.A \.set.cross./ (.B \.set.cross./ C.).)}
  . forAll:
    . a "in" A
    . b "in" B
    . c "in" C
    then: phi(((a, b), c)) = (a, (b, c))
Documented:
. called: "Canonical Associativity Bijection for Cartesian Products"
. description: "The monoidal associator isomorphism
                $\alpha_{A,B,C}: (A \times B) \times C \xrightarrow{\sim} A \times (B \times C)$.
                Demonstrates that while Cartesian products are strictly
                non-associative under set-theoretic encoding, they are naturally
                isomorphic in the monoidal category $(\mathbf{Set}, \times, 1)$."
Proof: """Define $\varphi : (A \times B) \times C \to A \times (B \times C)$ by
          $\varphi(((a, b), c)) = (a, (b, c))$. To show $\varphi$ is injective:
          if $\varphi(((a_1, b_1), c_1)) = \varphi(((a_2, b_2), c_2))$, then
          $(a_1, (b_1, c_1)) = (a_2, (b_2, c_2))$. By coordinate equality of
          ordered pairs, $a_1 = a_2$ and $(b_1, c_1) = (b_2, c_2)$, which
          further implies $b_1 = b_2$ and $c_1 = c_2$. Thus
          $((a_1, b_1), c_1) = ((a_2, b_2), c_2)$. To show $\varphi$ is
          surjective: for any $(a, (b, c)) \in A \times (B \times C)$, the
          element $((a, b), c) \in (A \times B) \times C$ maps directly to
          $(a, (b, c))$ under $\varphi$. Hence $\varphi$ is a bijection."""
Id: "cd332b77-e7cc-4e59-92e6-40decc503be8"
